Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 16:26:58 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: PPL: Many Types of Intellectual Property Are Not Protected

   From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)

   It's useful to remember--or to realize for the first time--that there
   are large areas where there is effectively no recognized intellectual
   property law. 

Some minor corrections:

   * Architecture. If I see a house design I really like, I am free to
   emulate it, be influenced by it, etc. Frank Lloyd Wright, for example,
   could not sue those who "used" his ideas.

The parts of a building that are visible from the public way are not
protected by copyright.  The remainder *is*.  And the purchase of the
building does not give one the right to make another building to the
same plans -- the copyright to the plans remains with the architect.
However, the purchaser a copy of the plans has the right to
build/possess *one* building to those plans, so if he tears down the
first, he can build a second to the same plans...  (Actually, it's
probably more complicated than that.)

   * Finally, fiction writers. They cannot stop others from being
   influenced by their writings. (The cases where it is ruled that a
   writer has a "right" to his "universe" and his "characters" is a
   disturbing trend. A recent court battle over someone's "sequel" to
   "Gone With the Wind," written about a century ago, typifies the
   absurdity of some aspects of this.)

Generally, writers have copyright in the characters they create.  (Why
do you find that disturbing?)

Dale

