Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 16:28:42 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: PPL

   From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>

   In my mind, patents are distinctly separate from copyrights.  Patents
   involve the use of scientific principles which were not created by the
   inventor; they were discovered.

   Copyrights, on the other hand, are different; they involve actual creations,
   not discoveries.

Actually, you can't patent a scientific principle, you can only patent
an invention, a device that uses the scientific principle.  If the
invention is the "only reasonable way" to employ a scientific
principle, it isn't patentable...

(Comp.patents has some reasonably good discussion of these issues!)

Dale

