Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 16:35:54 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: Market Cornering

   From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>

   Dale, are you seriously telling us that your great-grandfather purchased all
   the dried apples available (or all the stuff necessary to make dried
   apples), pushed the price artificially high, pulling in enough unearned
   money to cover those purchases, with enough left over to retire?  

(Oops, that's "my great-uncle", or perhaps great-great-uncle.)

No, the trick (apparently) is to buy enough contracts so that no-one
can get enough dried apples to the promised delivery points in time to
fulfil them all.  I'm not sure of the mechanics, having not studied
the financial/commodity markets, but it was a popular trick in the
late 1800's; I've seen numerous references to it.  Apparently it was
very tricky to pull off, but was very profitable if done successfully.

Whether one could consider the income "unearned" is a moral question,
not an economic one.

   The fact is, your ancestor made a lot of money selling dried apples.
   Period.  He had no more ability to "corner the market" than the Hunt
   brothers and for exactly the same reason.  (They don't call them "laws" of
   economics for nothing.)

Actually, I remember what screwed over the Hunt brothers, because I
was reading the newspaper accounts closely at the time: The Hunt's
system required that they continuously purchase more and more silver
futures contracts, as part of an interesting positive-feedback loop.
The futures exchange's governing body (I forget which one handles
silver) simply changed the rules, and limited each purchaser to 5,000
"contracts" per day.  Since this was less than what the Hunts were
already buying, their positive feedback loop reversed itself, and the
Hunts lost a billion or two.

The Hunt's weren't done in by the laws of economics, but rather by a
deliberate ambush by the (private) regulators of the exchange.
Whether the laws of economics would have done them in later, or
whether they would have been able to exit the scheme carrying a lot of
money, is something we can continue to argue about endlessly...

Dale

