Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 16:52:01 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: markets

   From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)

Trimming out a lot of stuff, and inserting what evidence I've been
able to track down:

   In
   particular, there is extremely detailed evidence of the conduct of
   commerce amung the sumerians far before the first coins appeared -- in
   fact, most cuneiform tablets that we have from the sumerians seem to
   be details of contracts and the like. I'd say that you are operating
   on a very weak foundation here. Barter was common long before the
   coining of money, and the market economy has likely operated since the
   first hominids appeared.

   Trade in finished goods appears to have predated writing, at least
   from what we can tell from pottery and other goods that appear to have
   been shipped comparitively long distances from their origins in the
   ancient middle east. The ground you are operating on is
   extraordinarily shakey. I'd go out and study some of the findings in
   archaeology in the last century or so before continuing on this line
   of reasoning.

My impression that there *was* long-distance trade for a long time in
the past, but it tended to be between rulers, as McEvedy (see below)
describes it, "Comecon-style".  The advent of independent merchants is
much more recent.

There *is* a lot of business record-keeping in ancient Egyptian,
Mesopotamian, and Phonecian writing.  But is it *contracts*, or is it
*bookkeeping*?  (And do the modern authors know the difference?)

But the critical question is not whether trade was done, but whether
the ordinary people got the goods for daily life by barter.  And it
seems that until relatively recently, they did not.  Much of past
trade was in "luxury" goods of one sort or another.

I've dug up some references that are worth reading, even if you don't
care about this particular dispute, because they seem to give some
idea of the historical basis and evolution of free-market systems:

Colin McEvedy, Penguin Atlas of Ancient History, in various places,
but especially p. 54, "375 B.C. Towns and Trade".

Encyclopedia Britannica (page numbers are the 1992 edition):
	"Culture", section "Nonurban cultures", vol. 16, pp. 881-888.
	"Economic Systems", section "Historical development", vol 17,
	pp. 906-908.

Herodotus, "Histories" (or "Persian Wars"), book I, section 94.

Unfortunately, all of these are of British extraction, so they may all
be tainted by the same misconceptions, but I think the evidence is
reasonably good:  It looks like the common people did not do the bulk
of their "economic" transactions in a market manner until the
development of reasonably modern urban societies.  It looks like
peasant-agricultural and earlier societies used a system of
socially-defined mutual obligations rather than market transactions.

Dale

